<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Balkans and Hungary</p><h1 class="st-title">Kosovo</h1><p class="st-meta">2008 CE – present · capital Pristina</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A landlocked Balkan state, its independence from Serbia declared in 2008 and recognised by many states but not by Serbia, which still claims it.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Kosovo declared independence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> on 17 February 2008, with partial diplomatic recognition. Serbia does not recognise it and claims it as its Autonomous Province of Kosovo and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metohija">Metohija</a>, although it accepts the governing authority of Kosovo's institutions under the 2013 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a> Agreement. The declaration followed the Kosovo War of 1998 and 1999 and nine years of administration by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a>, begun on 10 June 1999 under Resolution 1244.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Kosovo's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albanians">Albanians</a> had proclaimed a Republic of Kosova in 1990, recognised only by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albania">Albania</a>, and Ibrahim Rugova was elected its president in 1992. The Kosovo Liberation Army launched attacks from 1996, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a> bombed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a> from 24 March to 10 June 1999. Around 10,317 civilians were killed in the war, 8,676 of them Albanians, and large numbers of Albanians and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a> and other non-Albanians fled or were expelled. Talks led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martti-ahtisaari">Martti Ahtisaari</a> from 2006 did not produce a Security Council resolution. Ten states have since withdrawn recognition.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>In 2010 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-court-of-justice">International Court of Justice</a> found that the declaration of independence did not violate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-law">international law</a>. By 19 December 2025, 118 UN states recognised Kosovo, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, the United Kingdom and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> do not. Kosovo applied to join the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a> in December 2022. The 2024 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> recorded 93.5% of the population as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> Muslim.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kosovo.jpg" alt="Kosovo" width="330" height="236"><figcaption>Image: Cradel (current version), earlier version by Ningyou · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Kosovo.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>2008 CE – present</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Pristina</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Fatmir Sejdiu</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam, Eastern Orthodox, Catholic</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Albanian, Serbian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/kosovo">Kosovo</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Republic of Kosovo</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=2017&amp;focus=kosovo" aria-label="Kosovo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5192 661 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kosovo lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5512 917L5488 850"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5488 850h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5512 917h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5512 917h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Pristina. <a href="/atlas?year=2017&amp;focus=kosovo">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
